WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.230
Imagine a system outage on a weekday morning. Notices about

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.590
a service change now have to go out by phone,

3
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.830
today. Your team has never made calls at this scale,

4
00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.150
and people are anxious about getting it wrong. Everyone is

5
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.730
looking at you to see what happens next.

6
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.900
In that moment, I think most people want someone to

7
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.800
take charge and tell them what to do. So is

8
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.500
that what directive leadership is, someone stepping up and giving

9
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.120
clear orders?

10
00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.830
That is the heart of it. A directive leader sets

11
00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.330
the goal, the method and the deadline, assigns specific tasks,

12
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.630
and checks the work closely. The page describes it as

13
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.670
a style rooted in clarity and speed. In healthy organizations

14
00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.830
it is treated as a tool for particular situations, not

15
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:40.870
as a permanent personality.

16
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.380
People sometimes use the words directive and authoritarian as if

17
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.600
they meant the same thing. Is there a real difference,

18
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.320
or is it mostly a matter of tone?

19
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.670
There is a real difference. Authoritarian leadership is control for

20
00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.250
its own sake, where obedience becomes the goal. Directive leadership

21
00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.430
is about getting something done well when ambiguity is dangerous

22
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.650
or time is short. A skilled directive leader does not

23
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:08.170
use intimidation or loyalty tests. They step in decisively, explain

24
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.850
why, and step back once the moment has passed.

25
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.240
Let's go back to the outage. If I were on

26
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.200
that team with a strong directive leader, what would the

27
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.820
next hour actually look like for me?

28
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.520
You would get a clear plan: who calls whom, a

29
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.180
short script that includes a line about interpreters, and a

30
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.660
check-in every hour. You would know exactly what good looks

31
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:30.860
like and when it is due.

32
00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.730
And a thoughtful leader would add one more thing: telling

33
00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.030
the team this is a temporary approach for today, and

34
00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.810
that the next plan will be made together.

35
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.900
That sounds genuinely steadying. Beyond emergencies, where else does the

36
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.200
page say this kind of leadership is the right tool?

37
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.070
Sudden changes in law, high stakes audits, and moments when

38
00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:53.930
waiting for agreement could cause real harm. It also helps

39
00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.310
brand new staff, or people moving into unfamiliar technical work.

40
00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.850
Clear, step by step direction reduces overwhelm and prevents early

41
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.570
mistakes that can shake someone's confidence.

42
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.680
And the other side of the coin. What happens when

43
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.660
a leader uses this style all the time, even when

44
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:09.760
there is no crisis at all?

45
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:15.850
Experienced professionals start to feel micromanaged and undervalued. Over time,

46
00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.730
people stop thinking ahead and simply wait for instructions. The

47
00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.490
leader becomes the bottleneck, because the team can only move

48
00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:25.170
as fast as one person can review and approve every

49
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:25.490
task.

50
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:29.270
And there is a quieter risk: plans designed at the

51
00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.550
top, without hearing from the people doing the work, can

52
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.970
look sound on paper and fall apart in practice.

53
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.860
I like the idea of naming it out loud. It

54
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.420
seems to change how direction feels to the people receiving

55
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.860
it. Does the page give an example of what that

56
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:44.460
sounds like?

57
00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.830
It does. A leader might say: for the next thirty

58
00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.670
days we have a hard federal deadline, so I am

59
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.490
going to be very directive about workflow and daily check-ins.

60
00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.130
Once we clear it, we will look back together and

61
00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:57.150
redesign the process.

62
00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.570
Framing it as temporary and purposeful keeps trust while still

63
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.590
getting the speed and clarity of firm direction.

64
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.160
Let's turn to fair service and the experience of staff.

65
00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.240
The page calls directive leadership double-edged on equity. What makes

66
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.600
it double-edged rather than simply good or bad?

67
00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:17.930
It depends on what is being directed and when. Some

68
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.170
standards should never be up for a vote, such as

69
00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:26.870
accessibility requirements, timely responses to accommodation requests, and protections against

70
00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:27.170
harassment.

71
00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.050
A leader who says every public document will meet accessibility

72
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:35.110
standards before it goes out, no exceptions, can close gaps

73
00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:37.870
much faster than one who waits for everyone to agree.

74
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.140
There is also something in the page about new staff

75
00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.240
and unwritten rules. That one surprised me a little, because

76
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.720
direction is not usually described as a tool for fairness.

77
00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:52.430
Many workplaces run on expectations nobody ever writes down. Those

78
00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.030
hidden rules are hardest on people without insider networks, including

79
00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.130
staff who are the first in their families in this

80
00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.910
kind of work, neurodivergent colleagues and newer staff from underrepresented

81
00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:05.930
groups. Clear, explicit direction about what good work looks like

82
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.970
and how decisions move makes those rules visible to everyone.

83
00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.500
So where does it go wrong on equity? I imagine

84
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.740
it has to do with who gets told what to

85
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.960
do, and who gets trusted to decide.

86
00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:22.550
Exactly. Directive leadership harms equity in design work, when a

87
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.310
leader decides alone how an outreach plan or a program

88
00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:28.010
change should be built, without the people it affects. And

89
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.390
it harms careers when some staff are always told what

90
00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.070
to do while others get autonomy and coaching.

91
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.760
If that pattern follows race, disability or background, people are

92
00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.300
quietly locked out of the experience that leads to promotion.

93
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.400
For someone listening who leans on this style, maybe because

94
00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.520
they are good in a crisis, what does the page

95
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.320
suggest they try?

96
00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.070
Three things. Explain the reason behind every directive, so it

97
00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:55.010
feels purposeful rather than arbitrary. Look honestly at who on

98
00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.610
your team gets close direction and who gets room to

99
00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.870
decide, and ask whether that pattern is fair.

100
00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:04.350
And after a directive stretch, hold a short review where

101
00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.150
front line staff can safely say what worked, what created

102
00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.330
barriers and how to do it better next time.

103
00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.100
That makes directive leadership sound less like a personality and

104
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.700
more like a skill you choose to use. Which seems

105
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:18.900
like a hopeful way to look at it.

106
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:22.390
It is. Here is a question to carry with you.

107
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.590
Which of your directions this month were truly needed, and

108
00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.630
which were simply habit? Thank you for listening.
